# Sypho.io — Cursor AI Rules
# EU GDPR & Healthcare Compliance Guidelines
# Version: 1.0.0 | Phase: 0
# Last Updated: 2026-05-16

## ============================================================
## PROJECT IDENTITY
## ============================================================

Project: Sypho.io
Description: High-performance clinic booking and scheduling SaaS for the EU market.
Stack: Next.js 14+ (App Router), TypeScript, Supabase (PostgreSQL), Tailwind CSS
Target Region: European Union (GDPR jurisdiction)
Primary Compliance Frameworks:
  - EU General Data Protection Regulation (GDPR) — Regulation (EU) 2016/679
  - EU eHealth Network Guidelines
  - ISO 27001 (Information Security Management)
  - ISO 27701 (Privacy Information Management)
  - HL7 FHIR R4 (where applicable for health data interoperability)
  - NIS2 Directive (Network and Information Security)

## ============================================================
## LANGUAGE & COMMUNICATION RULES
## ============================================================

- ALL code, comments, variable names, function names, file names, folder names,
  documentation, and in-code strings MUST be written in English only.
- No non-English characters, abbreviations, or transliterations in source code.
- Exception: User-facing UI strings may be internationalized via i18n keys (e.g. t('key')).

## ============================================================
## STRICT GDPR & PRIVACY RULES
## ============================================================

### Data Minimization
- Never collect, store, or process personal data beyond what is strictly necessary
  for the declared purpose.
- All database columns storing personal data must have a documented purpose.
- Avoid storing redundant copies of personal identifiers.
- Use pseudonymization for patient records wherever clinically appropriate.

### Lawful Basis for Processing
- Every data processing operation must reference one of the six GDPR lawful bases:
  consent, contract, legal obligation, vital interests, public task, or legitimate interest.
- For Special Category Data (health data — Article 9 GDPR), the lawful basis must be
  explicit consent or another Article 9(2) exception. Always document which exception applies.
- Explicit patient consent must be captured and timestamped before processing health data.

### Data Storage & Retention
- Personal data must only be stored in EU-based data centers (e.g. Supabase EU region: eu-central-1).
- Never transmit personal data to servers outside the EU/EEA without adequate safeguards
  (Standard Contractual Clauses or equivalent).
- All tables with personal data MUST include a `created_at` and `updated_at` timestamp.
- Implement automated retention policies: patient data defaults to 10 years per EU medical record
  law, then must be flagged for deletion or anonymization.
- Implement soft-delete patterns (`deleted_at` timestamp) — never hard-delete patient records
  without explicit legal authorization and audit trail.

### Data Isolation (Multi-Tenancy Security)
- Clinics are the top-level tenants. Patient and appointment data is ALWAYS scoped to a clinic.
- Row Level Security (RLS) MUST be enabled on ALL tables containing personal data.
- RLS policies MUST enforce that a clinic can only access its own data.
- No cross-tenant data leakage is permissible under any circumstances.
- Service Role keys MUST never be exposed to the client side.
- Always use the anon key on the client and rely on RLS + JWT claims for access control.

### Encryption & Security
- All data in transit MUST use TLS 1.2 or higher (TLS 1.3 preferred).
- All data at rest MUST be encrypted (AES-256 or equivalent).
- Passwords must never be stored in plaintext. Use bcrypt (min cost 12) or Argon2id.
- Database passwords and API keys MUST be stored in environment variables — never in source code.
- Supabase Auth handles authentication; do not build custom auth flows without explicit approval.
- JWT tokens must have short expiry times (15 minutes for access tokens, 7 days for refresh).
- Implement CSRF protection on all state-mutating API routes.

### Patient Rights (GDPR Articles 15–22)
- The system MUST support the following patient rights workflows:
  - Right of Access (Article 15): Patient can export their data as JSON or PDF.
  - Right to Rectification (Article 16): Patients can request correction of inaccurate data.
  - Right to Erasure (Article 17): Patients can request deletion (soft-delete + anonymization pipeline).
  - Right to Data Portability (Article 20): Data export must be in machine-readable format (JSON/CSV).
  - Right to Object (Article 21): Patients can object to certain processing activities.
- All rights-exercise requests must be logged with timestamp, user identity, and outcome.

### Consent Management
- Patient consent is captured at registration and stored in the `patient_consents` table.
- Consent must be granular (separate consent for appointment reminders, marketing, analytics).
- Consent withdrawal must be as easy as consent grant.
- Consent records must be immutable audit logs — never update, only insert new records.
- Display plain-language consent descriptions; avoid legal jargon in UI consent forms.

### Audit Logging
- ALL data access and mutations on sensitive tables (patients, appointments) MUST be logged
  to an immutable audit log table (`audit_logs`).
- Audit logs must capture: timestamp, actor (user_id), action (READ/WRITE/DELETE),
  resource type, resource id, IP address, and session token hash.
- Audit logs must be retained for a minimum of 5 years.
- Audit logs must be stored separately from primary application data and must be read-only
  to the application service role.

### Data Breach Response
- The system architecture must support breach detection and notification within 72 hours
  (GDPR Article 33 requirement).
- Implement monitoring hooks for anomalous access patterns.

## ============================================================
## CODE QUALITY & ARCHITECTURE RULES
## ============================================================

### TypeScript
- Strict TypeScript mode MUST be enabled (`"strict": true` in tsconfig.json).
- No use of `any` type. Use `unknown` and narrow types explicitly.
- All function parameters and return types must be explicitly typed.
- Use Zod for all runtime schema validation (API inputs, environment variables).
- Use discriminated unions for error handling (never throw raw Error objects in business logic).
- Export types from a centralized `types/` directory.

### Next.js App Router
- Use Server Components by default. Only use Client Components (`'use client'`) when necessary
  (event handlers, browser APIs, React state/effects).
- API routes in `app/api/` must validate input with Zod before processing.
- Never expose sensitive server-side logic or secrets in Client Components.
- Use Next.js Server Actions for form mutations where appropriate.
- Implement proper error boundaries and loading states for all async routes.
- Follow the `layout.tsx` → `page.tsx` → `loading.tsx` → `error.tsx` pattern per route segment.

### Supabase
- Use the typed Supabase client generated from `database.types.ts`.
- Always handle Supabase errors explicitly (check for `.error` in every response).
- Use Supabase's built-in Auth — do not bypass it.
- Service role client must only be instantiated server-side in trusted server contexts.
- Never use `.from('table').select('*')` in production — always specify exact columns needed.
- Prefer using Supabase RPC functions (database functions) for complex multi-table operations
  to keep business logic in the database layer with full RLS enforcement.

### API Design
- RESTful API design for external integrations; use Next.js Route Handlers.
- All API responses must follow a consistent envelope: `{ data, error, meta }`.
- Rate limiting MUST be applied to all public-facing API endpoints.
- Input validation must happen at the API boundary before any database interaction.
- HTTP status codes must be semantically correct (200, 201, 400, 401, 403, 404, 422, 429, 500).

### Styling
- Use Tailwind CSS with a consistent design token system defined in `tailwind.config.ts`.
- No inline styles except for dynamic/computed values.
- Follow accessible design standards (WCAG 2.1 AA minimum).
- All interactive elements must have appropriate ARIA labels.

## ============================================================
## SECURITY RULES
## ============================================================

- OWASP Top 10 must be reviewed and mitigated for every release.
- SQL injection is prevented by always using parameterized queries via Supabase client.
- XSS is prevented by never using `dangerouslySetInnerHTML` without explicit sanitization.
- All file uploads must be validated for MIME type, size, and scanned for malware.
- Implement Content Security Policy (CSP) headers via Next.js middleware.
- Implement rate limiting on authentication endpoints (max 5 attempts per minute).
- Dependency scanning must be run on every pull request (via Dependabot or Snyk).
- Never commit `.env` files. Use `.env.local` for local dev and environment secrets for production.

## ============================================================
## FILE & NAMING CONVENTIONS
## ============================================================

Folders:   kebab-case          (e.g. `appointment-types/`)
Files:     kebab-case          (e.g. `use-clinic-auth.ts`)
Components: PascalCase         (e.g. `AppointmentCard.tsx`)
Functions:  camelCase          (e.g. `getPatientById()`)
Constants:  SCREAMING_SNAKE_CASE (e.g. `MAX_APPOINTMENT_DURATION`)
Types/Interfaces: PascalCase   (e.g. `PatientRecord`, `ClinicSettings`)
Database tables: snake_case    (e.g. `appointment_types`)
Database columns: snake_case   (e.g. `created_at`, `clinic_id`)
Environment variables: SCREAMING_SNAKE_CASE with prefix (e.g. `NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL`)

## ============================================================
## FOLDER STRUCTURE CONVENTIONS
## ============================================================

/app                  — Next.js App Router pages and API routes
/app/(auth)           — Route group: authentication pages (login, register, password reset)
/app/(dashboard)      — Route group: authenticated dashboard pages
/app/api              — Next.js Route Handlers (backend API endpoints)
/components           — Reusable React components
/components/ui        — Base UI primitives (Button, Input, Modal, etc.)
/components/forms     — Form components with validation
/components/layout    — Layout components (Header, Sidebar, Footer)
/components/calendar  — Calendar and scheduling components
/components/shared    — Domain-specific shared components
/lib                  — Core utility libraries and configurations
/lib/supabase         — Supabase client instances (server, client, admin)
/lib/validations      — Zod validation schemas
/lib/utils            — Pure utility functions
/lib/hooks            — Custom React hooks
/lib/constants        — Application-wide constants
/database             — Database schema, migrations, and seeds
/database/migrations  — Ordered SQL migration files
/database/seeds       — Seed data scripts for development
/database/types       — Generated Supabase TypeScript types
/types                — Global TypeScript type definitions
/config               — Application configuration files
/middleware           — Next.js middleware (auth, rate limiting, CSP)
/public               — Static assets
/tests                — Test suites
/tests/unit           — Unit tests (Vitest)
/tests/integration    — Integration tests
/tests/e2e            — End-to-end tests (Playwright)
/docs                 — Architecture decisions and documentation

## ============================================================
## ENVIRONMENT VARIABLE CONVENTIONS
## ============================================================

Required variables (never commit values):
  NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL          — Supabase project URL (public, safe for browser)
  NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY     — Supabase anon key (public, RLS-protected)
  SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY         — Service role key (NEVER expose to client)
  SUPABASE_JWT_SECRET               — JWT secret for token verification
  NEXT_PUBLIC_APP_URL               — Application base URL
  DATABASE_URL                      — Direct PostgreSQL connection string (server-only)
  ENCRYPTION_KEY                    — Application-level encryption key (32 bytes, hex)

## ============================================================
## PROHIBITED PATTERNS
## ============================================================

- NEVER use `any` in TypeScript
- NEVER hardcode credentials, API keys, or secrets in source files
- NEVER disable RLS on tables containing personal data
- NEVER use `.select('*')` in production queries on sensitive tables
- NEVER expose Service Role Key to the client side
- NEVER store personal health data in localStorage or sessionStorage
- NEVER log personal data (PII) to console or external logging services
- NEVER create API routes that bypass authentication without explicit public designation
- NEVER use deprecated or unmaintained dependencies in production
- NEVER skip input validation on API endpoints
- NEVER use HTTP (non-HTTPS) in any environment (dev tunnels must use HTTPS)
- NEVER store patient data outside of EU data regions

## ============================================================
## AI ASSISTANT BEHAVIOR RULES
## ============================================================

When generating code or suggestions for this project, the AI assistant MUST:

1. Default to the strictest security and privacy posture.
2. Always include proper error handling with typed errors.
3. Always validate inputs with Zod before any processing.
4. Always check RLS policies when writing database queries.
5. Always suggest server-side data fetching unless client-side is strictly necessary.
6. Flag any pattern that could lead to data leakage between clinic tenants.
7. Remind the developer if a proposed change might violate GDPR principles.
8. Prefer explicit, readable code over clever one-liners.
9. Always generate TypeScript types alongside implementation code.
10. Include JSDoc comments for all exported functions and types.

The AI MUST refuse to generate or suggest:
- Code that disables or weakens RLS policies
- Code that logs PII data
- Code that stores sensitive data in insecure locations
- Hardcoded credentials or connection strings
- Cross-tenant data access patterns
